    "result_text": "The build passes. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What I did\n\nExtended the registration-date business-day rule in `BusinessDays.rollForward` so an adjusted date landing on a listed public holiday rolls forward to the next non-holiday business day (previously it only skipped weekends).\n\n**`BusinessDays.java`** (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/`):\n- Added the fixed holiday calendar as a `Set<LocalDate>`: `2026-01-01, 2026-01-26, 2026-04-25, 2026-12-25, 2026-12-28`.\n- Changed the roll-forward loop condition from \"is weekend\" to \"is weekend **or** is holiday\", so it keeps stepping one day at a time \u2014 correctly handling chains where a holiday abuts a weekend (e.g. Fri 2026-12-25 \u2192 skips Sat/Sun \u2192 Mon 2026-12-28 is also a holiday \u2192 Tue 2026-12-29).\n\nBecause both call sites \u2014 `BuildOwner` (the persisted date) and `EnsureDailyOwnerLimit` (the daily-limit key) \u2014 go through this one method, both stay consistent. I also updated the now-stale doc comments in all three files.\n\nThe holiday list I used matches the `HOLIDAYS` set already pinned in the test base class (`AcceptanceBase.java`) exactly.\n\n## Tests\n\n`./mvnw -o test` \u2192 **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. The acceptance test `coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay` (which asserts the default registration date is never a weekend) passes.\n\nOne note: the specification's holiday behavior can't be driven black-box (it depends on the server clock and the fixed 2026 calendar), so there's no live test that lands a create on a specific holiday \u2014 the existing acceptance test only asserts the weekend invariant, as its own comment explains. The implementation nonetheless fully satisfies the specified rule.",
